<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<section xml:id="sec_ang_and_lin_vel">
        <title>Angular Velocity and Speed</title>
        <p>
                Using the relationship between angle and distance, we can now move to their <term>rates of change</term>, giving us a relationship between <term>angular velocity</term>, which is the rate at which the angle changes, and <term>speed</term>, which is the rate at which distance changes.
        </p>
        <p>
                        We will eventually move to calculating <term>linear velocity</term>, but because linear velocity is a <em>vector</em> quantity, what we will be calculating below is <term>speed</term>, which is the <em>magnitude</em> of the linear velocity. We will make the distinction clearer later in <xref ref="sec_ang_vel_problem"/>.
        </p>
        <exercise xml:id='exer_angular_from_linear'>
                <title>Finding Angular Velocity from Speed</title>
                <introduction>
                        <p>
                                        We will look again at person riding on a carousel that has a radius of <m>7</m>m.
                        </p>
                </introduction>
                <task>
                        <statement>
                                <p>
                                        If they have travelled 
                                        <m>14</m>m along the circumference of the carousel in <m>10</m> seconds,
                                        what is their speed (in meters per second)?
                                </p>
                        </statement>
                        <hint>
                                <p>
                                        Recall that speed is distance per unit time.
                                </p>
                        </hint>
                </task>
                <task>
                        <statement>
                                <p>
                                        If they have travelled 
                                        <m>14</m>m along the circumference of the carousel in <m>10</m> seconds,
                                                how many radians per second have they travelled? (This is their <term>angular velocity</term>.)
                                </p>
                        </statement>
                        <hint>
                                <p>
                                        Find the angle through which they have travelled and divide it by the number of seconds.
                                </p>
                        </hint>
                </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id='exer_vel_from_ang_vel'>
                <title>Finding Speed from Angular Velocity</title>
                <introduction>
                        <p>
                                        We will look again at person riding on a carousel that has a radius of <m>7</m>m.
                        </p>
                </introduction>
                <task>
                        <statement>
                                <p>
                                        When they have travelled through an angle of <m>\theta=1</m><nbsp/>radian in <m>10</m> seconds, what is their angular velocity?
                                </p>
                        </statement>
                </task>
                <task>
                        <statement>
                                <p>
                                        When they have travelled through an angle of <m>\theta=1</m><nbsp/>radian in <m>10</m> seconds, what is their speed (in meters per second)?
                                </p>
                        </statement>
                </task>
        </exercise>
</section>
